      "text": "To determine the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\( 400 \\), we start by using the formula for the sum of divisors of a number. The sum of the divisors of a \\( n \\)-th power of a prime \\( p \\) is given by \\( \\sum_{i=0}^{n} p^{i} = \\frac{p^{n+1}-1}{p-1} \\).\n\nGiven \\( 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\), we need to find the sum of the divisors of \\( 400 \\). The sum of the divisors function \\(\\sigma(n)\\) can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(n) = \\sigma(2^4) \\times \\sigma(5^2)\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's calculate \\(\\sigma(2^4)\\):\n\n\\[\nn = 2^4 \\implies \\sigma(2^4) = \\frac{2^{4+1}-1}{2-1} = \\frac{2^5-1}{1} = 32 - 1 = 31\n\\]\n\nNext, let's calculate \\(\\sigma(5^2)\\):\n\n\\[\nn = 5^2 \\implies \\sigma(5^2) = \\frac{5^{2+1}-1}{5-1} = \\frac{5^3-1}{4} = \\frac{125-1}{4} = \\frac{124}{4} = 31\n\\]\n\nNow, multiply the two results to get \\(\\sigma(400)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = \\sigma(2^4) \\times \\sigma(5^2) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the prime factorization of \\( 961 \\):\n\n\\[\n961 = 31^2\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 961 \\) is a perfect square of a prime number \\( 31 \\), the distinct prime factor is just \\( 31 \\).\n\nThus, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\( 400 \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
